## What is a General Investment Account?

A General Investment Account (GIA) (also known as taxable investment accounts and unwrapped investment accounts) is an account run by an investment [broker/platform](which-broker-should-i-use/) for you to buy and sell shares and funds. Capital gains within the account are subject to capital gains tax, and dividends and interest are subject to income tax.

## Why use one?

GIAs are generally used when contribution limits have been reached on more tax-advantaged accounts (like [ISAs](/isa/) and [pensions](/pensions/)), or when pension contributions are still possible, but the timeline of the investment doesn't match.

## What can I buy within a GIA?

You can generally buy any funds or shares that would be available in other accounts on the broker of your choice.

## How does the tax work?

It's best to think about a GIA like a bank account - you're not taxed just for having one, or on the funds contained within it, but only on the income (and capital gains in the case of GIAs).

### Income tax

Income tax is charged on both interest and dividends, but is very rarely taken at source, and so needs to be settled with HMRC separately, either via Self Assessment Tax Return or via your tax code, if HMRC allow that option.

Interest received from interest-bearing investments (typically bond funds) is charged at your usual income tax rate. 

Dividends received from funds or shares at the [current dividend rate](https://www.gov.uk/tax-on-dividends), with a £500 annual allowance before any tax is due.

You should receive a 'consolidated tax certificate' from your broker each year, in time for you to file a tax return, which shows all dividends and interest received in the previous tax year, and whether any tax was taken at source. It should provide all of the information needed to file a tax return.

:::note
Income tax is chargeable whether the income is reinvested within the funds or paid out.
:::


